  "raw_meta_yaml": "{% set name = \"spooles\" %}\n{% set version = \"2.2.0\" %}\n\npackage:\n  name: {{ name }}\n  version: {{ version }}\n\nsource:\n  fn: {{ name }}_{{ version }}.tar.gz\n  url: http://www.netlib.org/linalg/spooles/spooles.2.2.tgz\n  sha256: a84559a0e987a1e423055ef4fdf3035d55b65bbe4bf915efaa1a35bef7f8c5dd\n  patches:\n    - patches/gcc.patch  # [unix]\n    - patches/win.patch  # [win]\n    - patches/osx.patch\n    # Data races in the multithreaded factor and solve.  See the patch\n    # headers; without these spoolesMT.a returns silently wrong results.\n    - patches/0001-MT-add-acquire-release-primitives-and-order-the-work.patch\n    - patches/0002-MT-order-the-frontIsDone-p_mtx-publication-in-the-so.patch\n    - patches/0003-MT-make-the-solve-s-three-inter-phase-barriers-actua.patch\n    - patches/0004-MT-stop-losing-updates-on-the-factor-s-entry-counter.patch\n    - patches/0005-MT-give-each-solve-thread-its-own-cpus-vector.patch\n    # int overflow in the I2Ohash bucket index; Debian and FreeBSD carry\n    # the same fix.\n    - patches/0006-I2Ohash-fix-int-overflow-in-the-bucket-index.patch\n\nbuild:\n  number: 1006\n\nrequirements:\n  build:\n    - {{ compiler('c') }}  # [unix]\n    - {{ stdlib('c') }}    # [unix]\n    - {{ compiler('m2w64_c') }}  # [win]\n    - {{ stdlib('m2w64_c') }}    # [win]\n    - rsync  # [unix]\n    - make\n\ntest:\n  requires:\n    # Only where the test can actually run: the compiler package is built for\n    # the *host*, so on a cross-compiled target it is an executable the build\n    # machine cannot run (\"Bad CPU type in executable\").  conda-build does not\n    # skip the test phase when cross-compiling -- it runs the commands anyway.\n    - {{ compiler('c') }}  # [unix and build_platform == target_platform]\n  files:\n    - test_solve.c\n  commands:\n    - test -f ${PREFIX}/lib/spooles.a    # [unix]\n    - test -f ${PREFIX}/lib/spoolesMT.a  # [unix]\n    - if not exist %LIBRARY_LIB%\\spooles.a exit 1    # [win]\n    - if not exist %LIBRARY_LIB%\\spoolesMT.a exit 1  # [win]\n    # The MT race patches are only detectable at runtime probabilistically\n    # (see test_solve.c), so guard them deterministically as well: this\n    # header exists only if the MT patch series applied.  Catches the\n    # realistic regression -- a rerender or version bump silently dropping\n    # the patches -- with certainty and at no cost, on every platform\n    # including the cross-compiled and Windows ones.\n    - test -f ${PREFIX}/include/spooles/Utilities/SPOOLESatomic.h  # [unix]\n    - if not exist %LIBRARY_INC%\\spooles\\Utilities\\SPOOLESatomic.h exit 1  # [win]\n    # Actually solve a system and check the answer, serially and threaded.\n    # SPOOLES 2.2's MT layer had data races that returned silently wrong\n    # results -- no crash, no diagnostic -- which a \"does the .a exist\" check\n    # cannot see, and neither can SPOOLES' own drivers: they print an error\n    # metric and exit 0 whatever its value.  See the header of test_solve.c\n    # for the measured failure rates behind the iteration count.\n    - $CC $CFLAGS -o test_solve test_solve.c -I $PREFIX/include/spooles $PREFIX/lib/spoolesMT.a $PREFIX/lib/spooles.a $LDFLAGS -lpthread -lm  # [unix and build_platform == target_platform]\n    # 500 iterations at 8 threads: ~97% chance of catching an unpatched\n    # library on x86_64, for ~26 min on a 3-core runner against a 360 min\n    # timeout.  8 threads is the measured optimum -- detection falls off\n    # again by 16, where oversubscription serializes the interleavings it is\n    # meant to provoke.  The trailing argument is a wall-clock safety cap,\n    # not a tuning knob: it exists so a pathologically slow runner bails\n    # rather than burning the whole timeout.\n    - ./test_solve 500 8 20 2700  # [unix and build_platform == target_platform]\n\nabout:\n  home: http://www.netlib.org/linalg/spooles/spooles.2.2.html\n  summary: 'library for solving sparse real and complex linear systems of equations'\n  license: Public Domain\n  license_family: Public Domain\n  description: |\n    SPOOLES is a library for solving sparse real and complex linear systems of equations,\n    written in the C language using object oriented design.\n  doc_url: http://www.netlib.org/linalg/spooles/spooles.2.2.html\n\nextra:\n  recipe-maintainers:\n    - oursland\n    - looooo\n",
